From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 18:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Back PS5 fixed-address reservations with direct
 memory.

A program that reserves memory at an address of its choosing outside
Wine's reserved areas gets a bare sceKernelReserveVirtualRange
reservation. Committing it later only changes its protection, and the
console does not back a bare reservation when it is protected: the commit
succeeded and the first access faulted. Steam's protection layer on a
64-bit game does exactly this.

Give such a reservation to the direct-memory allocator, as the reserved
areas are, so a commit backs its pages; it is dropped from the allocator
again when the range is unmapped. PS5_Proton found the gap (mihawk-99,
its patch 0790).

  dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c | 4 ++++
  1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
index 4a37285..aab8ca3 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -851,6 +851,10 @@ static void *anon_mmap_tryfixed( void *start, size_t size, int prot, int flags )
         errno = EEXIST;
         return MAP_FAILED;
     }
+    /* Protecting a bare reservation does not back it with memory, so a
+     * later commit would fault on its first access: direct memory backs
+     * this range like the reserved areas (until it is unmapped). */
+    __wine_ps5_dmem_region( start, size );
     if (prot != PROT_NONE && anon_mmap_fixed( start, size, prot, flags ) == MAP_FAILED)
     {
         munmap( start, size );
